Fixed. Verified with a direct compile of the actual source against all relevant cases (the repo's `swift test` is blocked by a sandbox-exec restriction on this machine, so I compiled the real `NoteKit.swift` file directly instead).

**The bug** (`Sources/NoteKit/NoteKit.swift:5-7`): it split the body on spaces, then kept tokens with a `#` prefix. That meant any non-space delimiter (`,`) broke the word in half, and trailing punctuation (`.`, `)`) stayed glued to the tag, so `#urgent.` and `#ideas)` never matched.

**The fix**: match tags by pattern — a `#` followed by one or more word characters (letters, numbers, underscore) — using `NSRegularExpression` with unicode letter/number properties (`\p{L}\p{N}`). This ignores surrounding punctuation and preserves document order.

Verified behavior:
- `"hello #world #swift"` → `["world", "swift"]` (existing test, still passes)
- `"just plain text"` → `[]` (existing test, still passes)
- `"shopping list,#urgent."` → `["urgent"]` (was broken, now works)
- `"(#ideas)"` → `["ideas"]` (was broken, now works)
- `#b,#a #c,#d` → `["b", "a", "c", "d"]` (order preserved)
- `café #naïve #x` → `["naïve", "x"]` (unicode tags preserved)


